Name: Debugging Tools for Windows
Short Name: debugging_tools
URL: https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/drivers/debugger/debugger-download-tools
Version: 6.12.2.633 (default) and 10.0.26100.9457
License: Redistribution
License File: files\license.txt

Description:
The redistributable dbghelp.dll and symsrv.dll from Debugging Tools for
Windows, plus dbgcore.dll where the release has one.

This is how Sawbuck resolves the call stacks captured at each logging site.
dbghelp reads the PDBs; symsrv fetches them from a symbol server, which the
default configuration depends on -- viewer_window.cc builds a symbol path out
of two SRV* entries, and SRV* is what symsrv implements.

Two tiers, distinguished by the oldest Windows they run on, which is the only
axis that matters here:

  files\xp\{x86,x64}\     6.12.2.633        Windows XP and up
  files\win7\{x86,x64}\   10.0.26100.9457   Windows 7 and up

viewer.gyp selects one via the debugging_tools_tier variable (default 'xp',
override with -D debugging_tools_tier=win7) and its copy_dlls target puts the
pair matching the configuration being built next to the executable.
installer\sawbuck.wxs then ships whatever ended up there rather than reaching
into this directory itself, so the installer follows the architecture and the
tier automatically. See the comment on copy_dlls for why that selection has to
happen per configuration and cannot use GYP 'copies'.

Why there are two tiers:

  * 6.12.2.633 came from WDK 7.1 and imports only msvcrt.dll and
    kernel32.dll, so it needs no redistributable and runs from XP up. That is
    what makes it the default while XP support is a goal.
  * 10.0.26100.9457 imports the Universal CRT (api-ms-win-crt-*) plus the
    Windows 7 era API sets, including api-ms-win-downlevel-kernel32-l2-1-0.dll
    and api-ms-win-core-localregistry-l1-1-0.dll, which exist on Windows 7 and
    were removed in Windows 8. It needs the UCRT present, and in exchange it
    resolves essentially all of tip-of-tree Chromium's symbols.
  * 10.0.28000.2270 was vendored here briefly and removed: measured
    2026-11-16, it does not load on Windows 7 at all. It resolves a handful of
    functions 26100 cannot, which did not justify a third tier.

Note the Windows SDK's Debuggers directory is *not* versioned the way Include
and Lib are -- there is one Windows Kits\10\Debuggers\{x86,x64} and installing
a newer SDK overwrites it. Recovering an older build means extracting it from
that SDK's installer, e.g.
  winsdksetup.exe /layout C:\sdk26100
  msiexec /a "C:\sdk26100\Installers\X64 Debuggers And Tools-x64_en-us.msi" \
      /qb TARGETDIR=C:\dbg26100

dbgcore.dll exists only in the 26100 tier; 6.12 predates the minidump APIs
being split out of dbghelp. Nothing needs it: dbghelp's MiniDumpWriteDump and
MiniDumpReadDumpStream exports are forwarders to dbgcore, which the loader
resolves only for a caller that imports those two functions from dbghelp, and
neither Sawbuck nor Chromium base does. It is copied along with the tier for
completeness and is not shipped by the installer.

Not vendored: srcsrv.dll and srcsrv\pdbstr.exe. They were here previously and
were removed as unused. They implement *source* server, which fetches source
files out of version control for a source-indexed PDB, and Sawbuck never
displays source text. Not to be confused with symsrv.dll, the *symbol* server
client, which is required.

Local Modifications:
Copied from the Debuggers\x86 and Debuggers\x64 directories of a Windows SDK
installation (10.0.26100.9457) and of WDK 7.1 (6.12.2.633), arranged under
per-tier then per-architecture directories. The 6.12 32-bit pair is what used
to sit as a flat set of files directly in files\; it moved to files\xp\x86\
unchanged. srcsrv.dll and srcsrv\pdbstr.exe were dropped from both tiers.
